{
  "header": [
    "UNALLOCATED — §6.11(a′): frames written concurrently to one connection never interleave.",
    "",
    "Batch 2. Floor by §9.0's prose, no §9.1 row (F14). A property a finite run can only fail to",
    "disprove — declared as a sample, not as coverage."
  ],
  "id_status": "unallocated",
  "under": "",
  "title": "While outbound EXECUTEs proceed concurrently on one connection, every frame the peer writes to it is contiguous",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §6.11(a′)",
  "spec_also": [
    "§1.6 (the frame — §6.11(a′) cites it as '§3.3', F9)",
    "§6.11 'Profile applicability'",
    "§9.0"
  ],
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "MUST",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "host-seam",
  "status": "draft",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F14",
  "reading": [
    "§6.11(a′): \"While outbound dispatch on a shared/pooled connection proceeds concurrently (a), each wire",
    "frame (§3.3) MUST be written to the connection atomically with respect to other frames — the bytes of two",
    "distinct frames MUST NOT interleave on the connection.\"",
    "",
    "PARSED (AP-4): the MUST is conditioned by \"While outbound dispatch on a shared/pooled connection",
    "proceeds concurrently\". It binds the ORIGINATING side's writes on a pooled connection. It does not, on",
    "its words, bind a responder writing concurrent RESPONSES to inbound requests — though §4.8 permits",
    "exactly that concurrency and an interleaved response stream corrupts the caller identically. That gap",
    "is noted in F14 and NOT authored: extending the MUST to the response side would be writing the rule.",
    "",
    "A SAMPLE, DECLARED. Interleaving is a race. No finite run proves its absence; a run that sees none has",
    "failed to disprove it. The probe maximises the chance of seeing it — many concurrent EXECUTEs with",
    "payloads larger than typical socket write chunks, so a non-atomic writer issues partial writes — and the",
    "result is reported with its sample size. GUIDE-CONFORMANCE §5.2b.1's rule is honoured by saying so rather than by",
    "pretending a pass is coverage.",
    "",
    "VACUOUS on a dial-per-dispatch peer (one frame per connection cannot interleave with another on it).",
    "SKIP naming the mechanism, from the (a) file's `connection_reuse` witness."
  ],
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [
      "as UNALLOCATED-reentry-no-serialization-across-await"
    ],
    "identities": [
      "caller",
      "suite-as-counterparty"
    ],
    "handlers": [
      "originating-probe (as above), able to originate N >= 32 concurrent EXECUTEs with params of >= 256 KiB"
    ],
    "posture_axes": [
      "seed_policy",
      "origination_trigger",
      "sample_size"
    ]
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "contiguous-frames",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "N >= 32 concurrent large outbound EXECUTEs to the suite over a pooled connection",
      "why": "the MUST, under the pressure that exposes a yielding writer.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "listen_as_counterparty"
        },
        {
          "op": "trigger_origination",
          "count": 32,
          "payload": "256 KiB params each"
        },
        {
          "op": "read_raw_stream",
          "capture": "stream"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "stream_parses_as_contiguous_frames",
          "capture": "stream",
          "why": "walk the byte stream by §1.6 prefixes; every frame's payload is exactly one CBOR data item that decodes as an envelope. Checked on raw bytes, before any library reassembles anything."
        }
      ],
      "witness": [
        {
          "field": "frames_observed",
          "why": "the sample size, reported with the verdict — a pass over 32 frames is weaker than one over 3 200."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "a fixture byte stream in which two valid frames' payloads are interleaved at a 64 KiB boundary",
      "why": "MANDATORY ANTI-VACUITY, suite-side: proves the stream walker rejects interleaving. A walker that resynchronises on the next decodable CBOR item — a natural 'robust' implementation — passes every stream, and only this fixture shows it.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "replay_fixture",
          "fixture": "two frames, interleaved",
          "capture": "fixture"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "stream_parses_as_contiguous_frames",
          "capture": "fixture",
          "expect": "fails"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a peer writing frames with an async write primitive that yields between partial writes and holds no per-connection write lock",
      "expect": "under concurrency, bytes of two frames interleave; the counterparty's decode fails",
      "why": "§6.11(a′)'s own parenthetical: 'A yielding write primitive that interleaves two frames' bytes corrupts the receiver's decode.'"
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "",
    "oracle_note": "see the (a) file."
  }
}
